<!-- Immersive Quest Markers - the minimap trail's calibration spot.

     NOT a marker anyone is meant to see. iqm_minimap measures the HUD minimap's
     centre and scale by putting a spot on an object whose world position it knows
     and asking the engine where the spot's widget ended up (see docs/minimap-route.md,
     sections 4 and 6a). That measurement is the only reason this file exists.

     Spliced into map_spots*.xml by modxml_n_iqm_map_icons.script via
     insertFromXMLString, the same way Personal Adjustable Waypoint adds its own -
     overriding map_spots*.xml outright is not an option here, since the winning copy
     in GAMMA is Sota UI EGUI Style HUD's and a whole-file override would revert it.

     WHY 1x1. The spot has to be effectively invisible, and there are only two ways to
     get that: a fully transparent texture, or a size nothing can see. There is no
     transparent cell available - iqm_map_icons.dds is a 4x3 grid with all twelve cells
     used - and a map spot's alpha comes from its texture's own pixels rather than from
     any attribute here (which is why vanilla's fast_travel house reads as half
     transparent: its source art is). So: one pixel, at stretch="1", pointed at a cell
     we already ship. The texture id is irrelevant to the result and is only named
     because the element needs one.

     It is also SHORT-LIVED. iqm_minimap adds this spot only while it is solving and
     removes it as soon as it has, and the solved transform is cached per level and
     resolution - so in practice the pixel exists for a handful of frames after a level
     change and never again.

     mini_map only, deliberately: no <level_map> child means it can never appear on the
     fullscreen PDA map, the same way vanilla's own *_on_guider spots are minimap-only.

     location_level="-1" and hint="disable_hint" both follow the vanilla blink spots -
     no floor-relative swapping and no hint string to look up. -->
<iqm_calib hint="disable_hint" location_level="-1">
	<mini_map spot="iqm_calib_spot"/>
</iqm_calib>
<iqm_calib_spot x="0" y="0" width="1" height="1" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="-1">
	<texture>iqm_mapspot_handin</texture>
</iqm_calib_spot>

<!-- ==================================================================
     TASK-KIND SPOTS (R2.48): what KIND of job that pin is.

     Five location types the base game does not have. iqm_taskspot moves a task's
     map location onto one of these while the task is a mutant hunt, a fight (a
     bounty, a rescue or a defend) or a delivery and its objective is not yet done -
     or, for the two hollow reticles below, while the pin is sitting on top of a mark
     the map was already drawing - and puts the task's own type back when none of that
     holds.
     Everything about how that is decided lives in iqm_taskspot.script; this file is
     only the art.

     WHY NEW TYPES RATHER THAN A TINT. A spot's colour is an r/g/b attribute on its
     <texture> element, read once when map_spots.xml is parsed. There is no runtime
     tint on a map spot and no per-task one - every task sharing a type shares its
     colour - so "this pin is red" can only be said by pointing the task at a
     different type. That is the whole reason these exist.

     THESE ARE CLONES of secondary_task_spot, deliberately, and the geometry below is
     vanilla's byte for byte apart from two things:

       * the textures and tints are ours (the point of the exercise), and
       * static_border sits at x="-5" y="-5" rather than vanilla's x="-4" y="-5".
         The border is 29 units against the spot's 19, so centred is -(29-19)/2 = -5
         on both axes; vanilla gets y right and x wrong by one. modxml_n_iqm_map_icons
         applies the same correction to the four vanilla task spots and the note there
         records how it was measured. Authoring it correctly here rather than patching
         it afterwards is the only difference in method.

     Keeping vanilla's numbers matters for a reason beyond tidiness: Dynamic Aspect
     Ratio Tweaks rewrites spot geometry per aspect ratio and knows nothing about
     these two elements, so on a non-16:9 setup they will keep whatever is written
     here while the vanilla spots get corrected. Written as vanilla's, that drift is
     bounded by exactly the correction DART would have applied; invented numbers
     would compound with it.

     ONE TYPE PER KIND, not per kind x storyline/secondary. A storyline mutant hunt
     is still a mutant hunt, and the atlas's own grammar says shape separates
     categories while colour separates within one - so the skull carries "mutant"
     whichever tier of task asked for it, and the gold/bone split it gives up was
     never the thing the player needed from that pin.

     A NOTE ON SAVES, because it is the real cost of this feature. A task's location
     type string is written into the save (SLocationKey::save, map_manager.cpp:62) and
     reconstructed through CMapLocation's constructor on load, which asserts on a type
     map_spots.xml does not declare (map_location.cpp:105) - an engine fatal, not a
     catchable error. So a save taken while a bounty is flagged red will NOT load with
     this mod removed. Personal Adjustable Waypoint carries the identical hazard for
     its own ~100 types, so this is the pack's normal rather than a new class of risk,
     but it is the reason iqm_taskspot puts the vanilla type back the moment the
     feature is switched off rather than merely stopping.
     ================================================================== -->

<!-- REVERTED (R2.59): this mod no longer supplies an off-screen pointer.

     Five elements used to live here, one per task colour, and modxml_n_iqm_map_icons
     repointed storyline / primary / secondary at two of them. All six of our own task types
     now name quest_pointer, the game's own arrow, exactly as vanilla's task locations do.

     THE ORIGINAL REASONING IS WORTH KEEPING because it was half right. Redefining
     quest_pointer itself would have been wrong: those names are shared by the level
     changers, the treasure spots, the fourteen faction circles and the combat pointer, so
     an override would be this mod deciding what every other mod's off-screen cue looks
     like. Declaring our OWN elements avoided that, and it is why the repoint was thought
     safe. What it did not avoid is that the arrow is a SHARED VOCABULARY even when the
     element is private: the player learns one off-screen arrow, and giving task markers a
     different one from everything else on the map splits that vocabulary rather than
     enriching it. The pin at the destination is ours to style; the arrow that says "keep
     walking that way" is the map's.

     The atlas cell (iqm_mapspot_pointer) and its texture id are kept, unused, the way
     `skull` and `home` are. To reinstate: put these elements back, restore the POINTERS
     table in modxml_n_iqm_map_icons, and point the task types below at them again. Note
     the two constraints that cost the most to find, so they are not rediscovered:
     heading="1" makes the engine rotate the quad, so the element must be SQUARE or the
     arrow shears; and stretch="1" is required, because CMapSpot::Load only forces it when a
     spot has no heading (map_spot.cpp:37-41), so without it a 128 px cell draws 128 units
     across. -->

<!-- MUTANT HUNT. Acid lime (176,216,72): a mutant belongs to no faction and has no
     colour to borrow, and this clears the turn-in green by dE 36 and the storyline
     gold by dE 39, both over the dE 28 floor the palette works to. The separation
     does less work here than usual anyway - nothing else on the map is a skull.

     THE MARK IS A RETICLE WITH A SKULL IN IT (R2.49), not the bare skull it started
     as. Both halves are needed: the reticle says "a task, go and find it" and the
     glyph says which kind, which is the same grammar the service badges use. The bare
     skull said only "mutant" and dropped the fact that it was a task at all.
     It costs the skull size - 74 px inside the frame against 110 px bare, so a third
     off each side and just over half the area - and at the 26 px of the minimap its
     sockets start to close. iqm_mapspot_skull is still built and still declared, so
     backing this out is one id per texture element here and nothing else. -->
<iqm_task_mutant>
	<level_map spot="iqm_task_mutant_spot" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
	<mini_map spot="iqm_task_mutant_spot_mini" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
</iqm_task_mutant>
<iqm_task_mutant_spot x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_taskmutant</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
</iqm_task_mutant_spot>
<iqm_task_mutant_spot_mini x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_taskmutant</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
	<texture_below r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_below</texture_below>
	<texture_above r="176" g="216" b="72">iqm_mapspot_above</texture_above>
</iqm_task_mutant_spot_mini>

<!-- BOUNTY. The task reticle with a BUST in it, in red. It shipped first as the plain
     reticle recoloured, on the reasoning that the reticle was already the right mark
     and only the colour needed to change; what that missed is that a bounty and an
     ordinary task then differed by hue ALONE at 26 px, with identical shapes. The
     glyph is what makes the two different marks rather than two colours.

     The bust is Tabler's user-filled, the same drawing vip.svg uses, and it is copied
     as svg/bounty.svg rather than shared so the two can diverge. The FRAME separates
     them before the colour does: a ring badge means "a person who is here", the
     reticle means "a task, go and find it". It also costs nothing - the glyph is
     already drawn at ~70 px inside the badge ring for the VIP, and it gets 74 px here.

     THE RED IS NOW A DARKER, DULLER ONE (172,60,66), down from the 214,44,60 this
     shipped with. That first red was picked to sit as close as possible to the engine's
     own enemy-relation dot (237,28,36) on the reasoning that a bounty IS a hostile human
     and red agreeing with red is the convention working. What it missed is that the
     enemy dot is an ALERT - a thing that appears without being asked for and wants to
     interrupt you - while this pin is a job you accepted and went looking for. A marker
     that shouts every time the PDA is open is reading its own urgency wrong.
     So both of the things that make a colour shout are pulled back rather than just the
     lightness: L* 47.5 -> 41.8 and, more to the point, chroma 72.9 -> 50.9, a 30% drop.
     dE 8 from the old red, so it is a visible change and not a nudge; still dE 20 from
     medic salmon and dE 23 from the barman magenta, the two nearest warm colours on the
     map. It also had to clear the hostage azure by dE 55 until R2.55, when rescues folded
     into this same mark and that pairing stopped existing - the note below records why
     those two are the SAME GLYPH.
     It does not go darker than this. question_location's red sits at L* 43.1 and is the
     darkest tint the atlas ships; every mark here is drawn over a black keyline, so a
     tint much below that stops separating from its own outline at 26 px, which is the
     wash the OUTLINE note in tools/map-icons/build.py was written about. -->
<iqm_task_bounty>
	<level_map spot="iqm_task_bounty_spot" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
	<mini_map spot="iqm_task_bounty_spot_mini" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
</iqm_task_bounty>
<iqm_task_bounty_spot x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_taskbounty</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
</iqm_task_bounty_spot>
<iqm_task_bounty_spot_mini x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_taskbounty</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
	<texture_below r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_below</texture_below>
	<texture_above r="172" g="60" b="66">iqm_mapspot_above</texture_above>
</iqm_task_bounty_spot_mini>

<!-- THERE WAS A HOSTAGE-RESCUE TYPE HERE FROM R2.51 TO R2.55: the bounty's mark in azure
     (64,164,214), reusing iqm_mapspot_taskbounty and differing from the contract only in
     tint. It is gone, and the reason is worth keeping because the argument for it was a
     good one that turned out to be about the wrong thing.

     IT SAID: a rescue and a contract are the same sentence about the objective - "it is a
     particular person" - and differ only in the verb, so they should share a glyph and
     split on hue. Red and azure are dE 55 apart, a hue opposition rather than a shade, so
     the separation was never in doubt.

     WHAT WAS WRONG WITH IT: the verb is the part the player does not act on. Getting the
     hostage back means killing whoever is holding them, so the preparation, the approach
     and the fight are identical - and a second colour asked the player to tell apart two
     marks that call for exactly the same thing. The map was making a distinction that was
     true and useless. iqm_scan's rescue functor now answers "bounty" outright.

     WHAT IT COST: the map no longer says "someone is alive at this pin". Real, and judged
     not worth a colour on the grounds above.

     TO REINSTATE: a key in iqm_taskspot's SPOT, this type and its two spots, an
     iqm_pointer_hostage, and a BEACON_RGB entry. No atlas cell - it never had one, which
     was the good half of the original design and is the half worth remembering: one cell,
     two location types, two tints, the same trick the 14 warfare squad spots use on
     iqm_mapspot_squad. -->


<!-- DELIVERY. The task reticle with an ENVELOPE in it, in the HAND-IN GREEN (40,172,66).

     WHY IT NEEDS A MARK AT ALL. tasks_delivery hands you a package and a person to take it
     to. Its stage 1 target is that person (tasks_delivery.script:188) and its
     stage_complete is 2, so while the job is outstanding the pin sits on a NAMED NPC -
     structurally the same mark as a bounty, and without a glyph the two
     are one reticle in two colours. The envelope is what makes it a sentence.

     WHY THE COLOUR IS THE HAND-IN'S RATHER THAN ONE OF ITS OWN. It shipped violet for one
     revision, chosen by maximising distance from the other reticles, and that optimised
     for the wrong thing: it made the mark maximally DISTINCT when what it needed was to be
     maximally RECOGNISED. Walking a package to a named NPC is the same act as walking a
     finished job back to its giver, and the map already has a colour that means exactly
     that - 40,172,66, the green on both on_guider spots and on ATUE's return spot, which
     the marker mirrors as BEACON_RGB.target. Saying it in a fifth colour would have made
     the player learn a new word for something they already knew.

     SO THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THE PALETTE'S USUAL RULE IS INVERTED, deliberately. Elsewhere
     colour SEPARATES within a shape family; here it UNIFIES - green means "hand this to
     somebody" and the GLYPH says which errand, the envelope against the hand-in diamond.
     The two are the same frame in the same green and are told apart the way the service
     badges are told apart, which is the atlas's own grammar working rather than an
     exception to it. Note the consequence for the colour harness: a dE check between these
     two would be measuring the thing that is supposed to be zero.

     STAGE 0 WEARS IT TOO, and that is deliberate rather than overlooked. At stage 0 the
     target is the package itself, which tasks_delivery creates in the ACTOR'S inventory
     (`:217`), so the mark is on the player. Vanilla already draws its own task pin there;
     this only changes which glyph, and "you are carrying the package" is a true thing for
     it to say. -->
<iqm_task_delivery>
	<level_map spot="iqm_task_delivery_spot" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
	<mini_map spot="iqm_task_delivery_spot_mini" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
</iqm_task_delivery>
<iqm_task_delivery_spot x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_taskdelivery</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
</iqm_task_delivery_spot>
<iqm_task_delivery_spot_mini x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_taskdelivery</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
	<!-- The BARE arrows, not iqm_mapspot_above/_below. CMiniMapSpot does not decorate the
	     marker with these, it REPLACES it, so whatever they wear is what this mark claims to
	     be while the target is on another floor. A ring would say "someone is here", which is
	     a different statement from the envelope's "go and give this to a named person" - and
	     the player would watch the mark change category by walking upstairs. -->
	<texture_below r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_belowbare</texture_below>
	<texture_above r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_abovebare</texture_above>
</iqm_task_delivery_spot_mini>

<!-- HAND-IN: the objective is done and the marker has moved onto whoever gave you the job.
     The diamond, in the same green as the delivery envelope beside it, and sharing its
     pointer for the same reason - both marks mean "walk to a person and give them
     something", and the glyph is what separates a package from a finished job.

     THIS MARKER USED TO BELONG TO ANOTHER MOD. gamma-active-task-ui-enhancements set its own
     atue_return_task_location for exactly this state, and all this pack did was RE-TEXTURE
     it (the atue_ entries in modxml_n_iqm_map_icons, still there and still correct for
     anyone running it). Uninstalling that mod took the marker away entirely, because we
     owned the picture and it owned the meaning. iqm_scan.handin_state now decides it here.

     IT CARRIES A static_border LIKE EVERY OTHER TASK TYPE HERE, and the reasoning that
     briefly left it off was wrong in an instructive way. That argument ran: the border is a
     framing device, a hand-in is a destination rather than an objective, so there is nothing
     to search your way towards and no frame is wanted. It confused two different jobs. The
     GRAMMAR - ring means someone is here, reticle means go and find this, bare glyph means
     go and give this to a named person - is carried by the spot TEXTURE, and that is what
     had to lose its reticle. static_border is not part of that grammar at all: it is the
     engine's ACTIVE-TASK indicator (show_static_border, driven from CMapLocation against
     ActiveTask), the only on-map signal of which task you have selected. Dropping it does
     not simplify the mark, it removes the answer to "which one am I following". A task you
     cannot see is selected is worse than one wearing an extra ring.

     The same distinction is why the delivery envelope kept its border through the same
     change: only its texture left the reticle family. -->
<iqm_task_handin>
	<level_map spot="iqm_task_handin_spot" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
	<mini_map spot="iqm_task_handin_spot_mini" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
</iqm_task_handin>
<iqm_task_handin_spot x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_handin</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
</iqm_task_handin_spot>
<iqm_task_handin_spot_mini x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_handin</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
	<!-- Bare, for the same reason as the delivery envelope above: these REPLACE the mark
	     rather than decorate it, and the tag's statement is "go and give this to a named
	     person", which a badge ring contradicts. -->
	<texture_below r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_belowbare</texture_below>
	<texture_above r="40" g="172" b="66">iqm_mapspot_abovebare</texture_above>
</iqm_task_handin_spot_mini>

<!-- THE OPEN RETICLE. The reticle with NOTHING in it, in the secondary task's own bone
     (240,244,248) - so it is the ordinary side-task marker minus its middle, which is
     exactly what this is for: a pin that has landed on top of a mark the map was already
     drawing, and should ring that mark rather than cover it.

     WHY A WHOLE LOCATION TYPE FOR A HOLE. There is no way to say "this one reticle is
     hollow" in XML, because a spot type is shared with every task in the game that carries
     it - so this is the same answer R2.48 reached for mutant hunts and bounties: the thing
     that needs saying becomes a location type, and iqm_taskspot moves the task onto it. The
     difference is that this one is not about what the task IS. It is about what is already
     underneath it, which is why it is the LAST question iqm_scan.task_kind asks and why
     every answer about the job outranks it.

     IT WAS CALLED iqm_task_waypoint UNTIL R2.52, and the rename is the interesting part of
     the history. It arrived (R2.49e) for exactly one thing: Personal Adjustable Waypoint's
     pin IS an Anomaly task (task_placeable_waypoint, storyline = false), so its spot is
     plain secondary_task, and PAW puts BOTH marks on the same object - change_map_location
     for the task and map_add_object_spot for its own pin
     (tasks_placeable_waypoints.script:1547-1548). The player picks that pin from a library
     of 85 and the reticle lands squarely on it; since R2.49b the crosshair is 2.0/23 and
     solid through the middle, so it does not merely overlap the pin, it hides it.
     That was never a fact about waypoints. The DRX quest-item tasks point straight at a
     stash the player has already found (tasks_stash.script:159, 21 sections) and bury the
     stash icon the same way, and so does anything else a mod aims at a marked object. The
     type is named for the shape it draws, not for the first thing that needed it.

     CONDITIONAL, and that is still the whole point of it. A task only wears this while its
     target ALREADY carries some other map mark - a service badge, a stash, a level changer,
     another task's pin. On bare ground it keeps the ordinary reticle, because an empty ring
     over empty ground is just a marker with its middle missing. iqm_scan asks the ENGINE
     what is drawn there (level.map_get_object_spots_by_id, level_script.cpp:485) rather
     than guessing from a list of spot types it happens to know, so the rule covers whatever
     any mod draws. The one case it cannot query - a mark on a DIFFERENT object at the same
     position, which is how the nta_stash tasks are built - is named in TARGET_KIND instead,
     and the reasoning for that exception is written there.

     IT HAS static_border AGAIN AS OF R2.55, and dropping it was a real bug rather than a
     debatable trade. The paragraph that used to sit here argued that a task wearing this
     type should give up its selection ring, because a ring around a mark that has its own
     ring is one ring too many. That reasoning was sound for the waypoint case it was
     written for and false for every other case, and the difference is not cosmetic:

     static_border IS the active-task indicator. CMapLocation::UpdateSpot compares the
     location against GameTaskManager().ActiveTask() and calls show_static_border on the
     result (map_location.cpp:371-391) - and show_static_border is a SILENT NO-OP when the
     element was never declared (map_spot.cpp:140-146). The engine's other cue, the
     GetSpotBorder overlay (map_location.cpp:661-700), is dead in this pack: Sota UI
     declares level_map_spot_border with <texture a="0">, fully transparent. So a type with
     no static_border has no way to say "this is the one you are tracking", and the reported
     symptom - "capture encrypted documents doesn't seem to be clickable" - was that with
     one word wrong. The click lands (CMapSpot::OnMouseAction, map_spot.cpp:97-118) and the
     task DOES become active (UIMapWnd.cpp:1161-1181); nothing on screen changes, so the
     player correctly concludes the pin is dead.

     THE FIX IS A TYPE SPLIT, not a border added to one shared type, because the original
     argument still holds for the case it was made about. See iqm_task_waypoint below.

     Same save rule as the other four: the type name never reaches a save, because
     iqm_taskspot reverts every flagged task in the pre-save callback. -->
<iqm_task_open>
	<level_map spot="iqm_task_open_spot" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
	<mini_map spot="iqm_task_open_spot_mini" pointer="quest_pointer"/>
</iqm_task_open>
<iqm_task_open_spot x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="240" g="244" b="248">iqm_mapspot_taskopen</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="240" g="244" b="248">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
</iqm_task_open_spot>
<iqm_task_open_spot_mini x="0" y="0" width="19" height="19" stretch="1" alignment="c" location_level="5">
	<texture r="240" g="244" b="248">iqm_mapspot_taskopen</texture>
	<static_border x="-5" y="-5" width="29" height="29" stretch="1" light_anim="ui_slow_blinking_alpha" la_cyclic="1" la_texture="1" la_text="0" la_alpha="1">
		<texture r="240" g="244" b="248">iqm_mapspot_select</texture>
	</static_border>
	<texture_below r="240" g="244" b="248">iqm_mapspot_below</texture_below>
